{"word": "easily", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "easily", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adverb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiː.zɪ.li/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiːz.li/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation", "colloquial"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.zɪ.li/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.zə.li/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "easilier", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "more easily", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "easiliest", "tags": ["superlative"]}, {"word": "most easily", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Comfortably, without discomfort or anxiety.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Eftsoones she causd him vp to be conuayd, / And of his armes despoyled easily […]", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book II, Canto XI”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Why this already very fast train should be speeded up still further, when none of the other more easily timed S.R. West of England trains has a single minute pared from its schedule, is unexplained - unless this is a playful dig at the Western Region, most of whose expresses, by reason of additional stops, will be decelerated from the same date.", "reference": "1961 October, “The winter timetables of British Railways: Southern Region”, in Trains Illustrated, page 593:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "舒適地 /舒适地"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "mukavasti"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "helposti"}, {"language": {"code": "gl", "name": "Galician"}, "word": "tranquilamente"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "tranquillamente"}, {"language": {"code": "ml", "name": "Malayalam"}, "word